  "effectivePlan" : "We have six top tricks: two spades, two hearts and two clubs. Diamonds are the principal source of the three additional tricks; preserve dummy's club ace as an entry to established diamonds. Lead a low diamond toward the king, covering a significant West honor appropriately. If the king loses to the ace, regain control and lead the remaining diamond toward dummy, normally inserting the nine if West plays low; this helps develop the suit without unnecessarily spending the queen. If the king wins, use a heart entry to lead diamonds toward dummy again. Adapt to the honors and distribution revealed, establishing and cashing the long diamonds before spending dummy's final entry. A 3–3 diamond break should supply the needed three tricks after at most two concessions. Against a troublesome 4–2 break, reassess before conceding further tricks: a fourth spade may become a winner if spades divide 3–3, and a club finesse through West toward dummy's ten offers another potential trick. Preserve heart controls and South's club king as transportation; do not cash side winners prematurely. Prioritize nine tricks over overtricks and monitor how many defensive spades can be cashed after each concession.",
